   "title": "DNREC Pond Maps",
   "text": "DNREC Pond Maps\nDelaware Pond Maps\nOpen in a new window\nSmall Ponds\nState of Delaware Pond Locations\nMore than 30 Delaware freshwater lakes and ponds are open for fishing and other recreation. These impoundments, most of them managed by the Division of Fish and Wildlife, range in size from five to 189 acres. They support a variety of gamefish. The Division of Fish and Wildlife conducts an evaluation of the fish community in 6-8 ponds per year, thus each pond is surveyed approximately once every five years. Management actions (such as stocking or habitat improvement) are implemented as needed.\nFind full information about each of these ponds using the list and map resources on this page. It shows detailed maps of each water body, including bathymetric data such as depth, flow, areas with prominent vegetation and surface area to help anglers plan their fishing outings.\nThere are lists of fish species and their size distribution, information on the types of aquatic vegetation in each pond, and shoreline and boating access information.\nReturn to Delaware's Public Freshwater Fishing Ponds main page.\nPowered by Esri\nPublic Ponds\nAbbotts Pond\nAndrews Lake\nBecks Pond\nBlairs Pond\nChipman Pond\nConcord Pond\nCoursey Pond\nCraigs Pond\nDerby Pond\nGarrisons Lake\nGriffith Lake\nHaven Lake\nHearns Pond\nHorsey Pond\nIngrams Pond\nKillens Pond\nLums Pond\nMasseys Mill Pond\nMcColley Pond\nMcGinnis Pond\nMillsboro Pond\nMoores Lake\nMud Mill Pond\nPortsville Pond\nRaccoon Pond\nRecords Pond\nTrap Pond\nTrussum Pond\nTubmill Pond\nTussock Pond\nWagamons Pond\nWaples Pond\nOpen in a new window\nDNREC Division of Fish and Wildlife Pond Maps Viewer\nMore than 30 Delaware freshwater lakes and ponds are open for fishing and other recreation. These impoundments, most of them managed by the Division of Fish and Wildlife, range in size from five to 189 acres. They support a variety of gamefish.\nDon't show this again\nOK",
